  "surprise": "764 at 8 loops (743/815/734) against 509's 748 (755/761/728): +16, keep by the rule, not shown beyond noise: seeds 0 and 2 moved -12 and +6 and the mean gain is seed 1 (+54, 815, the highest single seed in this loop). The hypothesis was half right and the half that worked was the one I called secondary. Rounds rose less than computed: 13/14/12 (509: 11/11/11; predicted 14-17), so the pre-registered 'rounds did not rise' falsifier fired on two seeds. The fine-tune did halve (ft_steps 294-399 against 588-762), but late rounds only fell from 129-157 s to 102-133 s because the retry calls grew: the weaker one-loop pass sent more targets to 2/4/8-loop retries (2,091/1,836/1,992 retry-target slots against 1,754/1,580/1,671) and each pass returned more proofs to judge (Lean samples 452k/446k/433k against 404k/391k/398k). Fit got clearly worse, as the 'fine-tune volume matters' branch predicted: last-round 1-loop acceptance on RL targets 0.671/0.667/0.634 (509: 0.734/0.731/0.736; all three under my 0.70 line), 0.665/0.668/0.663 at matched round 11 (-0.07, predicted -0.02 to -0.05), round-2 acceptance 0.231/0.410/0.292 (509: 0.368/0.441/0.426), and per-sample dev accuracy fell by about 0.03 at both depths: 0.461/0.481/0.443 at 8 loops (509: 0.492/0.492/0.471) and 0.422/0.444/0.417 at 2 (509: 0.465/0.464/0.440). Yet that branch's metric prediction (below 735) did not follow: the model samples each proof less reliably and covers more theorems, 507's pattern again. The policy is visibly less peaked: new distinct proofs per late one-loop pass 546-608 (seed 0), 809-1,089 (seed 1), 757-847 (seed 2) against 509's 481-567, 419-438, 390-443, i.e. 1.3-2.5x more at similar solved counts, and distinct proofs handed back by the last pass 3,347/4,165/3,405 (509: 3,076/3,014/2,877). Cumulative RL solved 1,264/1,314/1,243 (509: 1,265/1,260/1,246): identical on seeds 0 and 2, +54 on seed 1, and the eval tracked it seed by seed. So two things moved together and this run cannot separate them: 2-3 more sampling passes, and 1.2-1.9 epochs per round instead of 2.4-3.7 leaving a higher-entropy policy; the per-seed pairing of RL solved with dev solved suggests the discovery side. Loops: metric minus diag shrank to +12 (12/20/5) from +21 (25/24/13), and on seed 2 metric and metric_diag (734 against 729) are within noise of each other, so this model is no better at 8 loops than at 2 there; per sample the 8-vs-2 gap is unchanged (0.039/0.037/0.026 against 0.027/0.028/0.031), the theorem gap is what narrowed. Retry crack rates pooled 15.1/18.1/19.0, 20.0/23.8/24.9, 15.3/17.0/17.1% at 2/4/8 loops (509: 11.4/14.6/15.3, 12.3/15.8/17.3, 11.7/13.7/14.9), higher because the failed sets are larger and easier (selection), and in rounds 3+ depth no longer orders them on seeds 0 and 2 (17.7/16.8/18.2 and 17.3/17.5/17.0; 509: 10.7/11.4/12.8 and 9.3/11.2/11.2). Pretraining identical up to nondeterminism: 3,409/3,470/3,422 steps, val at 2 loops 0.0824/0.0894/0.0912 (seed 2's pretrained model is the worst of the six and finished lowest). No forgetting: held-out greedy 0.984/0.986/0.985. RL charged 1,678/1,710/1,731 s with 122/90/69 s left; no crash. Caveat on the keep: like 509, one seed carries the mean. Next: the entropy reading says the fine-tune's epochs over the found proofs set the policy's peakedness and pass@64 pays for less of it; test it directly by holding rounds fixed and varying epochs (FT_RECORDS at one epoch of the mix, or RL_WEIGHT 4 -> 1), or raise sampling temperature in RL, and watch new-proofs-per-pass against per-sample accuracy. Depth did not get more valuable here and the gap in theorems got smaller.",
